From 7bccf61c86d4df08d1669617e4d84b1c7b29c29a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 05:13:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 66/66] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, the compositor's release fence is
 a GPU wait now
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 8 (Correct fence-based synchronisation at
 acquire and present)
Why: a zero-copy vkAcquireNextImageKHR waited for the compositor to
 finish reading the slot before it returned — nvMultiFenceWait on the
 calling thread, which is the thread about to record the frame. 0049's
 own comment measures what that costs through a swapchain acquire on a
 Nintendo Switch: 13.8 ms of a 16.7 ms frame while the GPU had 0.18 ms
 of work to do. Adding a fourth buffer did not move it, and would not:
 the wait is not for a buffer, it is for a read of one.
 .
 The dependency is real and this does not skip it. It moves it to where
 Vulkan already puts it — the semaphore and the fence the application
 passed to vkAcquireNextImageKHR, which are exactly the objects the
 specification says must be waited on before the image is used, and
 which the specification is explicit this call returning does NOT stand
 in for. The three patches before this one turn a foreign syncpoint
 fence into a vk_sync; this is the backend that produces one.
 .
 What that buys is not the wait getting shorter. It is the CPU being
 free during it: the application records and submits its frame while the
 host engine holds the channel at the syncpoint, and 0049 already made
 that wait cost the CPU nothing.
 .
 DECIDED ONCE PER SWAPCHAIN, from three things that cannot change
 afterwards: the zero-copy path (the copy fallback never receives a
 release fence — libnx's Framebuffer owns the slots and the dequeue
 happens inside framebufferBegin at present time), a driver that
 implements create_wait_sync, and MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT.
 A per-acquire decision would make every mean over a swapchain a mean
 over two different things. The decision is in the line the swapchain
 already logs, so a run says which shape it measured.
 .
 MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT=1 restores the old wait exactly,
 for the same reason HORIZON_GPU_FULL_BARRIER_WAITS exists a layer down:
 one console run can then compare the two shapes instead of comparing a
 number from this build against a number from another one. It is not a
 supported configuration.
 .
 The meter counts deferred acquires apart rather than folding them into
 the mean. A deferred acquire's total says when the image's INDEX became
 valid, not when the image became usable; averaging the two kinds
 together would report a number that is neither.
 .
 Two things this changes that are worth stating rather than discovering:
  - an application that acquires with neither a semaphore nor a fence
    now has nothing waiting for the compositor. That is invalid usage
    (VUID-vkAcquireNextImageKHR-semaphore-01780 requires one of them),
    and it was previously covered by accident.
  - the caller's timeout no longer bounds the release fence, only the
    dequeue. That is what the timeout is specified to bound.
Evidence: CROSS BUILD, scripts/ci-build-archives.sh in container mode over
 ghcr.io/d3fau4/nx-dev:latest: aarch64-none-elf-gcc (devkitA64) 15.2.0,
 ld.bfd 2.45.1, meson 1.11.2, -Wall -Wextra -Werror. The 877-target NVK
 build compiles wsi_horizon.c with no warning
 and links libvulkan_wsi.a; the pipeline ends with every archive built, 53
 .nro linking them, check-dispatch-complete OK (825 entry points) and
 check-tls-relocs OK.
 wsi_horizon_create_acquire_sync is in the archive, and t_vk_swapchain,
 t_vk_wsi_mt, t_vk_present_draw and t_nwindow are among the 53 .nro
 that link it.
 Also checked before that, and kept because it is a cheaper loop than a
 25-minute pipeline: `gcc 13.3.0 -fsyntax-only -std=c11 -Wall` on the
 host against Mesa's generated Vulkan headers and libnx's real headers from switchbrew/libnx dbcc1be; that check was
 confirmed to be a real one by making mf->fences[i].id a typo and
 watching it be rejected.
 A cross build is class X and CLAUDE.md is explicit that it never means
 working: NO CONSOLE HAS RUN THIS.
 .
 What a console run has to answer, in order:
  - correctness first. Whether a frame is ever rendered over a buffer
    the compositor is still reading, which shows up as tearing or as a
    torn band and not as any error. t_vk_swapchain, t_vk_wsi_mt,
    t_vk_present_draw and t_nwindow are the tests that would see it.
  - then the number. MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_ACQUIRE_STATS=1 with
    MESA_VK_NVKMD_HORIZON_SUBMIT_STATS=1, once as built and once with
    MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT=1, same resolution, same
    dock state, same cache state. The acquire line's "on the
    compositor's release fence" figure should go to nothing and its
    "handed that fence to the GPU" count to the frame count, while
    nvkmd_horizon's "wait(s) handed to the host engine" rises by one per
    frame.
Upstream: no — it is a Horizon-only backend using a Horizon-only driver
 hook.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 208 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 186 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index ebb3359..5c86692 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -694,6 +694,17 @@ struct wsi_horizon_image {
    NvMultiFence release_fence;
    bool have_release_fence;
 
+   /* True when the acquire that took this slot did NOT wait the release
+    * fence out, so it is still outstanding and belongs to whoever
+    * signals the application's acquire semaphore and fence.
+    *
+    * Written on every acquire, true or false, so it can never describe
+    * the previous one: the WSI runtime asks for the dependency
+    * immediately after the acquire returns, on the same thread, and the
+    * answer has to be about the dequeue that just happened.
+    */
+   bool release_fence_deferred;
+
    /* The region the graphic buffer told the compositor to read: the row
     * stride times the height rounded out to a whole block. Recorded at
     * registration rather than recomputed, so the decision can be
@@ -741,6 +752,13 @@ struct wsi_horizon_acquire_meter {
 
    uint32_t acquires;
    uint32_t rounds;          /* dequeues that answered "come back later" */
+   /* Of the acquires, the ones that handed the release fence on instead
+    * of waiting for it. Counted separately and not folded into a mean,
+    * because the two are not the same measurement: a deferred acquire's
+    * total says nothing about when the buffer became usable, only about
+    * when its index did. A window with a mix in it would report a mean
+    * that is neither. */
+   uint32_t deferred;
    uint64_t total_ns;
    uint64_t fence_ns;        /* of the total, inside nvMultiFenceWait  */
    uint64_t max_total_ns;
@@ -803,6 +821,18 @@ struct wsi_horizon_swapchain {
    /* Off unless MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_ACQUIRE_STATS asked for it. */
    struct wsi_horizon_acquire_meter meter;
 
+   /* True when the acquire hands the compositor's release fence to the
+    * driver instead of waiting for it here. Decided once, at creation,
+    * from three things that cannot change afterwards: the zero-copy
+    * path (the copy fallback never sees a release fence), a driver that
+    * implements wsi_device::horizon::create_wait_sync, and the
+    * MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT opt-out.
+    *
+    * Decided once rather than per acquire so that a swapchain behaves
+    * the same way for its whole life: a mixed swapchain would make
+    * every measurement of it a mean over two different things. */
+   bool gpu_acquire_wait;
+
    uint32_t image_count;
    struct wsi_horizon_image images[];
 };
@@ -1457,7 +1487,7 @@ wsi_horizon_extent_changed(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
 static void
 wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
                           uint64_t start_ns, uint64_t fence_ns,
-                          uint32_t rounds)
+                          uint32_t rounds, bool deferred)
 {
    struct wsi_horizon_acquire_meter *m = &chain->meter;
 
@@ -1481,6 +1511,7 @@ wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
 
    m->acquires++;
    m->rounds += rounds;
+   m->deferred += deferred ? 1u : 0u;
    m->total_ns += total_ns;
    m->fence_ns += fence_in_ns;
    if (total_ns > m->max_total_ns)
@@ -1496,11 +1527,12 @@ wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
              " acquire(s), mean %" PRIu64 " us (max %" PRIu64 "), of which "
              "%" PRIu64 " us waiting for a free buffer over %" PRIu32
              " dequeue round(s) and %" PRIu64 " us on the compositor's "
-             "release fence",
+             "release fence; %" PRIu32 " handed that fence to the GPU "
+             "instead of waiting for it",
              (now_ns - m->window_start_ns) / 1000000u, m->acquires,
              m->total_ns / m->acquires / 1000u, m->max_total_ns / 1000u,
              (m->total_ns - m->fence_ns) / m->acquires / 1000u, m->rounds,
-             m->fence_ns / m->acquires / 1000u);
+             m->fence_ns / m->acquires / 1000u, m->deferred);
 
    /* `enabled` survives the reset; everything the window accumulated
     * does not, so the next line is about the next second and not about
@@ -1508,6 +1540,7 @@ wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
    m->window_start_ns = 0;
    m->acquires = 0;
    m->rounds = 0;
+   m->deferred = 0;
    m->total_ns = 0;
    m->fence_ns = 0;
    m->max_total_ns = 0;
@@ -1578,26 +1611,46 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
          image->state = WSI_HORIZON_SLOT_ACQUIRED;
          image->release_fence = fence;
          image->have_release_fence = fence.num_fences > 0;
+         image->release_fence_deferred =
+            chain->gpu_acquire_wait && image->have_release_fence;
 
-         /* THE ONE CPU WAIT ON THIS PATH, and it is where CLAUDE.md
-          * puts one: swapchain acquire. The fence says when the
-          * compositor finished reading the buffer, and the application
-          * is about to render into it.
+         /* THE CPU WAIT THAT USED TO BE UNCONDITIONAL, and what took
+          * its place.
+          *
+          * The fence says when the compositor finished reading the
+          * buffer, and the application is about to render into it. That
+          * is a real dependency and nothing here skips it — the
+          * question is only who waits.
+          *
+          * Waiting here was where CLAUDE.md permits a CPU stall
+          * (swapchain acquire) and it was also the most expensive thing
+          * in the frame: 13.8 ms of a 16.7 ms budget while the GPU had
+          * 0.18 ms of work to do. The comment that used to be here said
+          * the dependency could not be expressed instead, because
+          * nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait performed a submit's waits on the CPU
+          * — moving the stall rather than removing it. 0049 made those
+          * waits happen on the host engine, and the three patches
+          * before this one carry a foreign syncpoint fence from here to
+          * there, so it can be expressed now.
           *
-          * Ideally this would become a wait dependency of the
-          * application's first submit instead. It cannot be, yet, and
-          * not because of this file: nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait performs a
-          * submit's waits on the CPU rather than emitting a GPU-side
-          * syncpoint wait, so expressing the dependency would move this
-          * same stall later without removing it. When cross-channel GPU
-          * waits exist, this becomes a vk_sync and the stall goes.
+          * So on the deferred path the fence is kept, not waited on,
+          * and wsi_horizon_create_acquire_sync below hands it to the
+          * driver as the payload of the semaphore and the fence the
+          * application passed to vkAcquireNextImageKHR. Nothing is
+          * signalled early: those two are exactly the objects Vulkan
+          * says the application must wait on before using the image,
+          * and the specification is explicit that this call returning
+          * does not mean the image is ready.
           *
-          * The timeout the caller asked for is honoured and the result
-          * is checked. The reference waits a fixed second and discards
-          * the answer, which turns a timeout into "go ahead and render
-          * over the buffer being scanned out".
+          * The wait below still runs where the fence cannot be handed
+          * on — the copy fallback, a driver with no create_wait_sync,
+          * MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT=1 — and is unchanged
+          * there. The timeout the caller asked for is honoured and the
+          * result is checked. The reference waits a fixed second and
+          * discards the answer, which turns a timeout into "go ahead
+          * and render over the buffer being scanned out".
           */
-         if (image->have_release_fence) {
+         if (image->have_release_fence && !image->release_fence_deferred) {
             const uint64_t spent_ns = os_time_get_nano() - start_ns;
             const uint64_t left_ns =
                timeout_ns > spent_ns ? timeout_ns - spent_ns : 0;
@@ -1629,7 +1682,8 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
             }
          }
 
-         wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(chain, start_ns, fence_ns, rounds);
+         wsi_horizon_meter_acquire(chain, start_ns, fence_ns, rounds,
+                                   image->release_fence_deferred);
          *image_index = (uint32_t)slot;
          return VK_SUCCESS;
       }
@@ -1799,6 +1853,11 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_fallback(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
          continue;
 
       chain->images[idx].state = WSI_HORIZON_SLOT_ACQUIRED;
+      /* There is no release fence on this path — the compositor never
+       * hands one to a producer that copies — so there is nothing to
+       * defer. Written rather than left alone, on the same rule the
+       * field's comment states: it describes THIS acquire. */
+      chain->images[idx].release_fence_deferred = false;
       chain->next_image = (idx + 1) % chain->image_count;
       *image_index = idx;
       return VK_SUCCESS;
@@ -1825,6 +1884,84 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_fallback(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
    return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
 }
 
+/* WHAT THE APPLICATION STILL HAS TO WAIT FOR, handed to the runtime as
+ * a vk_sync so the GPU can wait for it instead of this thread.
+ *
+ * Called by wsi_common_acquire_next_image2 once for the semaphore and
+ * once for the fence the application passed, immediately after the
+ * acquire returned and on the same thread — which is why
+ * `release_fence_deferred` is written on every acquire and can only ever
+ * describe the one that just happened.
+ *
+ * VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT means "there is nothing outstanding", and
+ * it is the ordinary answer on three of the four paths through here: the
+ * copy fallback, a compositor that released the slot with no fence, and
+ * an acquire that waited the fence out itself. The caller then signals
+ * the application's semaphore immediately, which is what it did before
+ * any of this existed and is correct in all three cases.
+ *
+ * A FAILURE IS NOT DOWNGRADED TO THAT. If the fence was deferred and the
+ * driver cannot take it, the dependency is real and outstanding and
+ * nobody has waited for it; answering FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT would signal
+ * the semaphore anyway and let the application render into a buffer the
+ * compositor is still reading. So the error goes back, vkAcquireNext-
+ * ImageKHR fails with it, and the application can act on it. The path
+ * that can actually happen here is an out-of-host-memory in
+ * vk_sync_create; a driver with no create_wait_sync never sets
+ * `gpu_acquire_wait` in the first place.
+ */
+static VkResult
+wsi_horizon_create_acquire_sync(const struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
+                                const struct wsi_image *wsi_image,
+                                struct vk_sync **sync_out)
+{
+   /* Both are the first member of their subclass, which is what every
+    * other cast in this file relies on. */
+   const struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain =
+      (const struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *)wsi_chain;
+   const struct wsi_horizon_image *image =
+      (const struct wsi_horizon_image *)wsi_image;
+
+   if (!image->release_fence_deferred)
+      return VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT;
+
+   /* `gpu_acquire_wait` is only ever set where this is non-NULL and
+    * `release_fence_deferred` only where that is set, so this cannot
+    * fire. It is written out because the alternative to a check is a
+    * call through a null pointer, and because the answer if it ever did
+    * fire must NOT be FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT: a fence was deferred and
+    * nobody has waited for it. */
+   if (chain->base.wsi->horizon.create_wait_sync == NULL) {
+      mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: a release fence was deferred to a driver "
+                "that cannot take one");
+      return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
+   }
+
+   const NvMultiFence *mf = &image->release_fence;
+   const uint32_t count = mf->num_fences;
+
+   /* num_fences comes from the compositor and indexes a fixed array, so
+    * it is bounded here rather than trusted. A count that does not fit
+    * is not truncated: dropping one fence is a dependency never waited
+    * for, and the acquire has not waited for any of them. */
+   if (count == 0 || count > ARRAY_SIZE(mf->fences)) {
+      mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: the compositor released a slot with %"
+                PRIu32 " fences, which is not a number this can carry",
+                count);
+      return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
+   }
+
+   struct wsi_horizon_fence fences[ARRAY_SIZE(mf->fences)];
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+      fences[i].syncpt_id = (uint32_t)mf->fences[i].id;
+      fences[i].threshold = (uint32_t)mf->fences[i].value;
+      fences[i].valid = true;
+   }
+
+   return chain->base.wsi->horizon.create_wait_sync(chain->base.device,
+                                                    fences, count, sync_out);
+}
+
 static VkResult
 wsi_horizon_swapchain_acquire_next_image(struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
                                          const VkAcquireNextImageInfoKHR *info,
@@ -2616,6 +2753,7 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_create_swapchain(VkIcdSurfaceBase *icd_surface,
       chain->base.get_wsi_image = wsi_horizon_swapchain_get_wsi_image;
       chain->base.acquire_next_image =
          wsi_horizon_swapchain_acquire_next_image;
+      chain->base.create_acquire_sync = wsi_horizon_create_acquire_sync;
       chain->base.release_images = wsi_horizon_swapchain_release_images;
       chain->base.queue_present = wsi_horizon_swapchain_queue_present;
       chain->base.wait_for_present = wsi_horizon_swapchain_wait_for_present;
@@ -2700,10 +2838,36 @@ done:
     * is deliberate: the debug-utils messenger is how an application
     * observes the present path, which is the whole reason Phase 6 asked
     * for the decision to be observable at run time. */
+   /* WHO WAITS FOR THE COMPOSITOR, decided once and for this swapchain's
+    * whole life.
+    *
+    * Three conditions, none of which can change afterwards:
+    *   - the zero-copy path. The copy fallback never receives a release
+    *     fence at all — libnx's Framebuffer owns the slots there and the
+    *     dequeue happens inside framebufferBegin at present time.
+    *   - a driver that implements create_wait_sync. Without it the fence
+    *     cannot become a vk_sync and the only place left to wait is
+    *     here.
+    *   - the opt-out. MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT=1 restores
+    *     the CPU wait exactly, so one console run can measure the two
+    *     shapes against each other instead of comparing a number from
+    *     this build with a number from another one. It is the same
+    *     device HORIZON_GPU_FULL_BARRIER_WAITS serves a layer down, and
+    *     it is not a supported configuration.
+    *
+    * Decided here rather than per acquire so a swapchain behaves one way
+    * throughout: a mixed one would make every mean over it a mean over
+    * two different things. */
+   chain->gpu_acquire_wait =
+      chain->zero_copy && wsi_device->horizon.create_wait_sync != NULL &&
+      !debug_get_bool_option("MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT", false);
+
    vk_logi(VK_LOG_OBJS(&log_device->base),
-           "wsi_horizon: %s (%u images, %ux%u, swap interval %u)",
+           "wsi_horizon: %s (%u images, %ux%u, swap interval %u); the "
+           "compositor's release fence is waited for by %s",
            chain->decision, image_count, extent.width, extent.height,
-           chain->swap_interval);
+           chain->swap_interval,
+           chain->gpu_acquire_wait ? "the GPU" : "the acquiring thread");
    if (!chain->zero_copy && why != NULL) {
       vk_logi(VK_LOG_OBJS(&log_device->base),
               "wsi_horizon: zero-copy was declined because %s", why);
-- 
